module v2.workflow.floor_non_verdict


// THE NON-VERDICT ROSTER: identities enrolled as expected-red that the required floor EXECUTES
// and that produce NO BOOLEAN VERDICT -- they throw, or they answer with something that is not a
// Bool -- enrolled by exact qualified name.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS, and the fact it exists to make gating rather than merely visible. `cli_run`
// `run_required_floor` routes such a claim to `known_red_runtime_errored` or
// `known_red_observation_unreadable`, and those arms are HONEST: they say correctly that this
// enrolled claim produced no verdict. The defect was never in the arm. It was in the
// COMPOSITION -- `claim_executor` `required_floor_outcome_is_clean` consulted neither, so the
// floor returned CLEAN while an enrolled expected-red assertion had ceased to assert anything.
// A diagnostic can describe the evidence truthfully while the gate draws a false conclusion
// from it, and that is what happened here on every run for a day.
//
// THE RUNG, PER SEAM, because the governing rung is the minimum across them and stating only
// the strongest seam is the inflation DESIGN section 4b(1) forbids: routing a thrown claim into
// its arm is MITIGATABLE; printing the count is MITIGATABLE; printing `failed=0` without
// distinguishing verdict incompleteness is a MISLEADING PROJECTION; and returning CLEAN while
// an enrolled claim produced no verdict is BELOW FLOOR. Below floor is not a rung -- it is
// outside the ladder and forbidden outright -- which is why this roster gates rather than
// waiting for the population to be repaid first.
//
// WHAT ENROLMENT ADMITS, AND WHAT IT CANNOT. Enrolment in `floor_expected_red` is an admission
// about a known SEMANTIC VERDICT: this witness reaches its subject and answers false. It is not
// permission for the subject to stop evaluating. Without this roster the expected-red arm
// absorbs an arbitrary regression INSIDE the enrolled population -- yesterday the witness
// executed and returned false, today it throws before reaching its assertion, no verdict
// exists, and the floor stays clean. (Strictly inside it: an UNENROLLED witness that throws
// goes through the ordinary failure path and already gates. The absorption was never repo-wide,
// and saying otherwise would overstate the defect this closes.)
//
// WHAT THIS WALL CONFINES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. Stated first because the short name for this
// module -- a growth freeze -- claims more than the mechanism delivers, and a reader who takes
// the name literally will conclude that growth is REFUSED. It is not, in one direction.
//
// CONFINED, by execution, every run: the OBSERVED population must equal the ROSTER exactly. An
// observed identity the roster does not carry refuses (unrostered debt); a rostered identity the
// run did not observe refuses (stale exemption). Together those pin O = R.
//
// NOT CONFINED: the roster itself. Nothing here compares the roster authored at HEAD against the
// roster admitted at the MERGE BASE, so an author whose change makes a NEW identity stop
// producing a verdict may add its row IN THE SAME CHANGE. Observed then equals roster, both
// arms pass, and the regression is admitted -- LEGALISED BY THE ROSTER THAT EXISTS TO BOUND IT.
// The row becomes the permission, which is exactly the defect review 55361 identified for stale
// rows, arriving through authorship rather than through survival.
//
// THIS IS A CONSEQUENCE OF THE SINGLE-RUN DESIGN AND NOT AN OVERSIGHT IN IT. The admission was
// specified as `added = head_observed - base_observed`, which needs a SECOND floor run over the
// base. Substituting the roster for base-observation is what makes the wall implementable in one
// run -- and that substitution is sound exactly to the degree the roster cannot be grown freely.
// So `R subset-of B` is the condition that makes the simplification safe, not an extra nicety on
// top of it, and its absence is the honest cost of the cheap design.
//
// RUNG ON THAT AXIS: MITIGATABLE. Roster growth is confined by review diligence alone, which is
// strictly weaker than execution, and this paragraph is the declaration rather than a reader's
// inference.
//
// NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: the floor establishes `H subset-of B` -- the roster in the COMPOSED RESULT is a
// subset of the roster at that result's CURRENT TARGET-BASE PARENT. NOT the merge base, and the
// difference is a real admission rather than a spelling: with M = {a} at the merge base, main
// repairing `a` and deleting its row (B = {}), and a stale branch reintroducing it (H = O = {a}),
// a subset-of-M rule PASSES and readmits an exemption main already retired. The mirror is a false
// red on a row main legitimately added after the fork. The cut therefore needs the composed result
// and its base parent, which a branch-head-only run cannot supply.

// THE ADMISSION AS IMPLEMENTED: the ADDED set is empty.
//
//     added  = head_non_verdict_identities - roster
//     repaid = roster - head_non_verdict_identities
//     admit  <=> added is empty
//
// So 142 -> 43 -> 42 -> 0 is admitted and 142 -> 143 refuses. So does one old identity repaired
// while a different identity begins throwing, with the count still reading 142 -- and THAT is
// why both sides are compared as IDENTITY SETS and never as counts. A repaired witness must not
// buy permission for an unrelated witness to lose its verdict, and a count-based implementation
// admits exactly that trade in silence. The corpus has already paid for this lesson once in the
// other direction: the false regression that produced this module was a comparison whose
// `planned` counts matched on both sides while the trees did not.
//
// REPAYMENT AND ROSTER DELETION ARE ONE ACT, and an earlier revision of this header said the
// opposite. It argued that refusing a stale row "punishes the fix", because gunbc#9020 repairs
// 99 of these in one landing and would go red for it. That argument is wrong, and review 55361
// found why: A STALE ROW IS A LIVE EXEMPTION. The identity is repaired, the row stays, and if
// that witness later stops producing a verdict again it is ALREADY ROSTERED -- so the exact
// regression this wall exists to refuse is admitted in silence. Repayment without deletion turns
// a bounded debt row into a permanent licence, which is the absorbing fallback wearing the word
// "diagnostic".
//
// Refusing does not punish a repair; it requires the repair to be COMPLETE. The run names every
// row to delete, the deletion is mechanical, and this is the discipline `floor_route_gap` and
// the expected-red staleness join already enforce. The asymmetry was the error and consistency
// with them is the repair -- which is worth recording, because the asymmetry had an argument
// behind it and was endorsed before a reviewer found the hole in it.
//
// EMPTY IS THE INTENDED TERMINAL STATE. When every enrolled identity reaches its subject and
// answers, this module deletes, and the two collections become ordinary conjuncts over nothing.
//
// THE POPULATION, MEASURED RATHER THAN ESTIMATED (floor run 32694337921, sha 1239cace120): 142
// identities, all of them `known_red_runtime_errored` -- `known_red_observation_unreadable` was
// 0 on that run, so every member of this population threw rather than answering a non-Bool. The
// causes:
//    119  no declaration named X in this execution's loaded index
//     13  type error
//      9  undefined variable
//      1  call contract mismatch
//
// THE DOMINANT CAUSE IS AN AUTHORED DEFECT, NOT A FLOOR SCOPE DEFECT, and it was checked rather
// than assumed: four of the unresolved names were looked up and every one IS declared in the
// corpus, while the witness referencing it declares no import that would bring it into the run's
// loaded index. The refusal is correct and the witness is unimportable, so the repair is a per
// witness import and never a widening of the floor's scope.
